//
// GENERATED by tools/lua-port-gen/lua2des.mjs from
// nethack-c/recorder/dat/hellfill.lua. Regenerate rather than hand-edit; the
// call stream is re-checked against the .lua by
// test/lua-port-scripts.test.mjs on every run.
//
// The script spends RNG of its own — percent(), shuffle() or math.random(),
// all of which are nhlib.lua's shims over NetHack's rn2(). Equivalence is
// therefore "the same des.* calls with the same arguments *and* the same
// rn2() draws in the same order"; js/lua-js/nhlib.mjs is where the draw
// sequences are defined, and --check pins them against the .lua.
//
// `contents`/`inventory` closures become arrow functions. The bridge pushes
// them with lua_pushcclosure(), and a C function pointer is a JS function
// object in this transpile, so the C binding calls back into the port at
// exactly the point it would have called the Lua closure.
//
// The .lua assigns `hells` without `local`, so in the interpreter it
// lands in the script state's globals. Nothing ever reads it back — the state
// is torn down when the level is finished — so the port makes it an ordinary
// binding.
//
// `function hellobjects()`, `function hellmonsters()`, `function helltraps()`, `function populatemaze()`, `function rnd_halign()`, `function rnd_valign()`, `function rnd_hell_prefab()` is a *global* function in the
// interpreter, for the same reason and with the same consequence: the script
// state dies with the level, so a plain JS declaration is exact.
//
// Original header:
//   NetHack 5.0	hellfill.des	$NHDT-Date: 1432512783 2015/05/25 00:13:03 $  $NHDT-Branch: master $:$NHDT-Revision: 1.25 $
